  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency humanitarian support for earthquake-affected population in northwest Syria (NWS)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A magnitude 7.7 earthquake struck central T&#xFC;rkiye and northwest Syria (NWS) on 6 February 2023, that were further followed with more than 1,200 aftershocks, causing widespread damage. According to OCHA, as of 9 February, seven million individuals are estimated to have been affected by the earthquake across Aleppo and Idleb Governorates. Thousands are reported to have died and many more have been injured the numbers are rising by the hour and the situation is grave amid the harsh winter conditions. The disaster took place during a harsh winter with negative temperatures, snowfalls, and floods. Affected families face urgent needs including shelter, non-food items (NFI) such as blankets, mattresses, hygiene items, and food.

Within this project, IOM will support 1,400 households representing approximately 12,400 individuals (2,480 men, 2,480 women, 3,720 boys, 3,720 girls) through shelter and non-food items (NFI) kit distributions. The standard NFI kit (in-line with local Shelter/NFI Cluster recommendations) contains a kitchen set, a hygiene kit, mattresses, blankets, jerrycans, plastic sheets, solar lamps, diapers, and carpets for a family of five. 

IOM will procure the relief items through a competitive tendering process or by using IOM existing long-term agreements (LTAs). IOM selected two implementing partners (IPs), namely, Takaful el Sham and Shafak, who will assess and distribute the relief items to earthquake-affected households. IOM will work in Aleppo and Idleb Governorates (exact locations/sub-districts to be determined), in coordination with the S/NFI Cluster, to undertake household-level registration for the most vulnerable populations eligible for tent and NFI assistance. 

Within this project, IOM will also utilize part of the SCHF funds to cover IPs&#x2019; costs for distributions and transportations of in-kind donations received by IOM from international and private donors. Most in-kind donations are delivered to Gaziantep International airport or Adana airport. IOM will arrange the logistics to deliver the aid from the donor handover location to the cross-border checkpoints. As IOM is informed of incoming donations, IOM will assign a sub-IP (or multiple sub-IPs depending on the number of items, the capacity of the partners, and the type of items donated) for their distribution. The list of sub-IPs that will be engaged into the distribution of in-kind items includes: HIHFAD, Syria Relief, Violet, IYD, Big Heart, Muzun, and Watan.</narrative>
  </description>
